Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Stamen-Grafted Wire
- Story & Context: In the bleak mid-winter of 1997, engineer Nikolai spliced a hot-wired melodica straight into the primary longwave transmitter at the Ostrov Beacon, intent on drowning out local military chatter with pure sonic defiance. The resulting broadcast overloaded the relay station's transformer, turning the night sky a static-charged violet while passing cargo ships logged unexplainable sub-bass reverberations for forty miles around. Local radio pirates caught the initial blast on tape, capturing the precise moment an acoustic reed instrument collided with 100,000 watts of overdriven valve power. "Turn the dial till the copper bleeds white," rasped an anonymous operator over the track's crushing, 2-step garage shuffle drop.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: 35mm film grain visual of a bright green botanical plant wire-grafted directly into a glowing red vacuum tube inside a rusty radio transmitter, sparks flying into dark, freezing sea mist with raw text overlays.

Track 2: Rootstock / Svalbard
- Story & Context: Produced entirely inside an abandoned botanical research station near Longyearbyen, this track captures vocalist Elena Rostova using her melodica to play mournful, pitch-bent melodies against a furious 136 BPM Future Bass beat. The session was interrupted when security forces raided the station, mistaking the low-frequency bass oscillations for seismic drilling equipment. Elena held the line, improvising a scathing vocal hook directly into an old carbon-button telephone microphone while the equipment was pulled from the walls. "You can kill the stem but the frost remembers the root," she spat into the receiver, creating the scene's most iconic vocal sample.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Stark photographic artwork showing a frozen greenhouse covered in thick polar ice, illuminated from within by an angry, pulsing red strobe light, casting high-contrast shadows across snowy mountain terrain.

Track 3: The Gardener of Station 9
- Story & Context: Legend holds that after the Ostrov Beacon was permanently shuttered in 1999, a ghost signal continued to broadcast this track every night at midnight on 198 kHz. Built on a ghostly sample of wind howling through broken glass layered over a devastating, distorted 808 drop, the song became an anthem of urban myth among Scandinavian ravers. Modern crate-diggers still hunt for the original, unmastered DAT tape allegedly pulled from the wreck of a supply boat that ran aground while chasing the frequency. "Listen close to the fog, we left our seeds in the iron," whispers a pitched-down voice as the track fades into pure vacuum-tube hiss.
